description:
Implement and FREEZE Contract 1 of the four contracts the plan says must be locked before any feature code (section 29.1).

WHY THIS IS FIRST: Section 29.1 states 'If these four are right, everything above (bounty/battle/guild) stays clean and pluggable. If any is wrong, stop features and fix the contract.' The Extension API is the surface every feature is written against, so a wrong shape here poisons every downstream bead. Section 29.2 reinforces: core stays EXACTLY the section 20 runtime and knows NO Bounty/Battle/Guild/XP.

FILE: packages/core/src/contracts.ts. Per plan section 20:

export interface CommandHandler<T = unknown> { type: string; handle(cmd: Command<T>, ctx: RuntimeContext): Promise<GameEvent[]>; }
export interface EventHandler { on: string; handle(evt: GameEvent, ctx: RuntimeContext): Promise<void>; }
export interface Reducer<S> { feature: string; initial: S; reduce(state: S, evt: GameEvent): S; }
export interface Capability { name: string; description: string; }
export interface GameFeature {
  id: string;
  commands?: CommandHandler[];
  eventHandlers?: EventHandler[];
  reducers?: Reducer<unknown>[];
  capabilities?: Capability[];
  requires?: string[];   // capability names; missing -> degrade with warning
}

ALSO the supporting primitives in packages/core/src/:
- entity.ts: Entity{id, kind, version, createdAt, updatedAt}
- event.ts: GameEvent{type, occurredAt, actorId, causationId?, payload}
- command.ts: Command{type, issuedAt, issuerId, payload}
- state.ts: StateStore get/update + namespaced per-feature stores
- contracts.ts (above)
- registry.ts: command registry + capability registry
- persistence.ts: persistence boundary interface (Neon adapter implements)
- index.ts barrel

HARD CONSTRAINTS:
- Core contains ZERO game words: no Bounty, Guild, Battle, XP, Level, Quest, Pet, Inventory, Reputation. A grep for these in packages/core must return nothing.
- features[] is explicit composition (section 29.2): createRuntime({extensions: [...]}). NO dynamic plugin loader, NO plugin marketplace, NO remote/dynamic plugin loading, NO distributed event infra, NO microservices. All of those are BANNED for MVP.
- Section 12.3 lineage (earendil-works/pi): 'Do not build the feature into the core. Build the core so the feature can be built outside the core.' Core is difficult to change, easy to extend.

CONSIDERATIONS: requires[] degradation is the key extensibility mechanism. A feature whose dependency capability is missing must WARN AND DEGRADE, never hard-fail the runtime. This is what lets the removal test pass and lets Battle run without Reputation.

SUCCESS CRITERIA: the interface compiles; a sample no-op feature installs and uninstalls; core greps clean of game words; the contract is marked frozen in a comment so later beads treat changes as breaking.

TESTS: unit tests for the type shapes (compile-time), a test that a feature declaring requires:['nonexistent.cap'] installs with a warning rather than throwing, and a test asserting core/ source contains no game vocabulary.

PLAN INCONSISTENCY TO RESOLVE BEFORE FREEZING (found during bead-graph review):
The plan states the feature contract in TWO places that do not agree.

(a) Section 12.2 says: 'Feature contract: {id, commands, eventHandlers, reducers, capabilities, effects}; runtime.install/uninstall.'
(b) Section 20 GameFeature has: {id, commands?, eventHandlers?, reducers?, capabilities?, requires?} - there is NO 'effects' field, and it does have 'requires'.

RESOLUTION REQUIRED IN THIS BEAD, not deferred: section 29.1 names section 20 as canonical ('Extension API (section 20 GameFeature + createRuntime) - frozen first'), so default to (b) and treat 'effects' in section 12.2 as an earlier sketch. But do not silently drop the concept - decide explicitly whether effects are needed (timers, background scheduling, out-of-band side effects) and either add a typed 'effects' member or record that reducers plus event handlers fully cover it. A frozen contract that silently disagrees with another section of the plan is a trap for the first third-party extension author.

SECOND INCONSISTENCY, same class: the per-feature FILE layout also differs.
(a) Section 29.2: 'each extension is commands/events/state/domain/index'
(b) Section 24: 'domain.ts, commands.ts, events.ts, reducer.ts, rules.ts, repository.ts, index.ts, README.md, *.test.ts'
Section 24 is more detailed and includes rules.ts, repository.ts, tests, and a README - all load-bearing (rules.ts holds the XP tables and the behaviour-to-build classifiers; repository.ts is what keeps persistence per-feature). Adopt (b), and note that 'state' in (a) corresponds to the reducer plus the namespaced StateStore slice rather than a separate file.

THIRD, SECTION 33 EXTENDS THE LAYOUT AGAIN: features 'gain two optional dirs: application/ (command/query handlers = the single domain implementation all surfaces share) and cli/ + mcp/ contribution fragments ONLY if the interface-adapter pattern needs per-feature metadata (default: pure registry entries, no code)'. Reconcile this with (b) so application/ is a documented optional subdirectory of the section 24 layout rather than a competing scheme.

WHY THIS MATTERS ENOUGH TO CALL OUT: the whole extension architecture is justified by third parties building on it (sections 12, 33, M7). An ambiguous contract turns a contributor into a guesser, and the third-party-adapter-proof bead exists precisely to catch that. Better to resolve it here, before the contract is declared frozen.

Implement the core runtime, event bus, and the anti-overengineering event-boundary rule from plan sections 20, 29.2, 29.3.

FILE: packages/core/src/runtime.ts, per plan section 20:

export function createRuntime(opts: { extensions: GameFeature[]; store: StateStore; bus: EventBus; log?: Logger }) {
  const commands = new Map<string, CommandHandler>();
  const handlers = new Map<string, EventHandler[]>();
  const caps = new Map<string, string>(); // capability -> feature id
  for (const ext of opts.extensions) {
    for (const c of ext.commands ?? []) { if (commands.has(c.type)) throw new Error('duplicate command ' + c.type); commands.set(c.type, c); }
    for (const h of ext.eventHandlers ?? []) pushToList(handlers, h.on, h);
    for (const cap of ext.capabilities ?? []) caps.set(cap.name, ext.id);
    const missing = (ext.requires ?? []).filter(r => !caps.has(r));
    if (missing.length) opts.log?.warn('[core] feature ' + ext.id + ' degraded, missing: ' + missing.join(','));
  }
  return {
    capabilities: [...caps.keys()],
    async dispatch(cmd: Command) {
      const h = commands.get(cmd.type); if (!h) throw new Error('unknown command ' + cmd.type);
      const evts = await h.handle(cmd, ctx);
      for (const e of evts) await this.emit(e);
      return evts;
    },
    async emit(evt: GameEvent) {
      await opts.store.append(evt);   // event_log, key events only (see section 7.2 filter)
      for (const h of handlers.get(evt.type) ?? []) await h.handle(evt, ctx);
      opts.bus.publish(evt);          // SSE/WS fan-out, transient allowed
    },
    uninstall(id: string) { /* remove feature handlers; removal-test hooks here */ },
  };
}

ALSO: packages/core/src/lifecycle.ts (start/stop, graceful shutdown).

THE EVENT-BOUNDARY RULE (section 29.3) - this is the important design constraint, encode it in review docs and tests:
- Events are the CROSS-FEATURE INTEGRATION BOUNDARY ONLY.
- Direct domain operations (createBounty(), validateBounty(), calculateReward()) stay PLAIN FUNCTION CALLS inside their feature.
- Cross-feature side effects (BountyCompleted -> +XP / +rep / achievement check) go through the bus.
- If EVERY function call becomes an event, the system is overengineered - reject in review.

ORDERING CONSTRAINT inside emit(): persist to event_log FIRST (durability), then run handlers, then publish to the realtime bus (transient fan-out). This ordering matters because section 30 makes the activity log a first-class P0 feature that replay, audit, achievements, reputation, and anti-abuse all read from.

CONSIDERATIONS: duplicate command types must throw at install time, not silently overwrite - a duplicate is almost always a composition-root bug. uninstall() must fully deregister commands, handlers AND capabilities, or the removal test gives false confidence.

SUCCESS CRITERIA: dispatch/emit/install/uninstall all work; uninstall fully deregisters; capability degradation warns rather than throws; no game vocabulary in core.

TESTS: unit test that dispatch emits the handler's returned events in order; test that a duplicate command type throws; test that uninstall removes capabilities from the registry; test that a degraded feature still handles its own events.
